  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 scholarly book is already showing early signs of use, with 962 recorded downloads and open-access availability through an academic repository. Its record also indicates alignment with Quality Education and support from KU Leuven, suggesting institutional backing and relevance for teaching and scholarly study.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">An Opaque Mirror For Trajan is a recent scholarly monograph by Laurens van der Wiel from Leuven University Press that is beginning to find an audience through direct access and sustained download activity. The strongest signal in the current record is usage: 962 downloads were recorded, with a pronounced early peak followed by continued month-by-month readership. In impact terms, that pattern points to immediate scholarly attention and active consultation rather than merely archival availability.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book is also openly accessible in a repository, which lowers barriers to reading and can support dissemination beyond the publisher’s site. That access profile matters for a work in classics and ancient history, where readership often develops gradually through specialist communities, libraries, and teaching use. The record further notes a connection to Quality Education, indicating a plausible educational dimension to the book’s reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the broader citation record remains early. OpenAlex currently shows no citations, so there is not yet evidence here of downstream scholarly uptake through formal referencing. That should be read as a limited maturity of the citation trail rather than as a judgment on the work’s significance; books in the humanities often accrue cited impact over a longer timescale than usage signals appear.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The available metadata also suggests credible scholarly infrastructure around the publication. KU Leuven is listed as a funder, and the book is associated with open-science peer review signals in the record. Together with the repository presence, these details point to a work that is positioned for visibility, reuse, and classroom or research-library discovery, even while its citation footprint is still developing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence portrays a book with clear early readership and open dissemination, but a still-emerging formal citation profile. For a specialist monograph, that is a meaningful stage: the book is accessible, being downloaded, and situated within an educational and research-oriented publishing context, with scholarly uptake likely to become clearer over time.</p>",
